Hi Andrew,

(1) Indeed, the solution is to split up your predictor term into two separate non-linear parameters (with corresponding predictor terms), so that you can set a boundary on one of them. As an alternative to setting boundaries via set_prior, you could also write exp(sitex) in your non-linear formula, which ensures the resulting effect is always positive.

For instance, you may want your discrimination alpha to be positive only, which is trivially achieved by exp(alpha).

(2) Indeed, the ID-syntax is the way to go. For instance,

alpha ~ 0 + (1|i|site),
beta ~ 0 + intercept + site_x + (1|i|site)

