In this case, you should not write the samer survey answer into multiple rows or else you will overestimate your information.

Your data seems like a particularily complex case of multi-membership models, where each response belongs to multiple platforms, publishers, etc. at the same time.

I that correct?

If you have, for instance, two publishers belonging to the same response, you can go for

y ~ imps + (1 | mm(Publisher1, Publisher2))

If the effects of imps should vary across publishers:

y ~ imps + (1 + imps | mm(Publisher1, Publisher2)) or

y ~ imps + (1 + mmc(imps1, imps2) | mm(Publisher1, Publisher2))

depending on whether imps has different values across publishers. For the latter, you will need the dev version of brms from github.

