Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I had considered the prior, but I might have misunderstood their role. I assumed the parameter priors are included in the WAIC since it's a Bayesian AIC , and hence also present in the posterior probabilities. Since the default in post_prob is equal model priors, I assumed that it should be like WAIC weights. I think I found some papers by Gelman and a couple textbook chapters that might help elucidate the difference though. Thanks!

On Monday, March 12, 2018 at 1:27:15 PM UTC-4, Paul Buerkner wrote:

> post_prob computes its weights based on Bayes factors, which are in turn computed from marginal_likelihoods based on bridgesampling.
>
> BFs are conceptually related to BIC but only vaguely so and the relation between BFs and "true" information criteria such as AIC and WAIC is even more complex.
> Thus, we would definitely expect different results, especially if you didn't think of priors (I assume you didn't think of priors), because BFs are heavily influenced by priors.

> 2018-03-12 17:55 GMT+01:00 Brandon Vaughan <ilikes...@gmail.com>:

> > ?post_prob mentioned it was based on the marginal likelihoods, so I figured it was related to bayes factors but given that compare_BIC gives about the same schwarz rates I figured it'd also yield posterior probabilities based on bayes factors (since BIC is 2*log(BF) ) for the OLS fit models consistent with brms's post probs. It's more of a conceptual question I suppose that might have been suited for stack exchange. Just curious as to what is different in the assumptions made in AIC/BIC/WAIC/Akaike Weights from the bridge sampling of marginal likelihoods that leads to such different conclusions about the best model.
> >
> > On Monday, March 12, 2018 at 4:07:12 AM UTC-4, Paul Buerkner wrote:
> > > post_prob computes weights based on Bayes factors not information criteria which is something fundamentally different. see ?post_prob
> > >
> > > maybe I just make a method that allows to compute all kinds of weights.
> > >
> > > >
> > > >

