From ude6fb7786d893b5c at groups.google.com  Tue Mar 20 00:57:21 2018
From: ude6fb7786d893b5c at groups.google.com (Travis Riddle)
Date: Tue, 20 Mar 2018 00:57:21 +0000
Subject: Possible error when specifying `by` variables in grouping term
Message-ID: <ZpVWyq1FuwY.g-ZeSrsLCAAJ@brms-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/brms-users/c/ZpVWyq1FuwY/m/g-ZeSrsLCAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Paul,

I think I may have discovered a problem that can arise when specifying `by` variables in the grouping term. The short version is that it looks like when the identifying values contain periods, the levels get mixed up in estimation. Below is a reproducible example illustrating the issue:

#Data frame containing the "person-level" data
df  <- data.frame(ID = rep(seq(1,200)),
                  condition = rep(c('Treatment', 'Control'), each=100),
                  group = rep(c('A', 'B'), 100),
                  grp_mean = rep(0, 200))

#specify that the standard deviations vary by group
df_sd <- data.frame(condition=c('Treatment', 'Treatment', 'Control', 'Control'),
                    group = c('A', 'B', 'A', 'B'),
                    grp_sd = c(.5, 2, .5, 2))

df %>%
  left_join(df_sd) %>%
  mutate(grp_cond = paste(group, condition, sep='_')) %>%
  mutate(person_mean = rnorm(n(), mean=grp_mean, sd=grp_sd)) %>%
  mutate(ID_2 = sample(c('.V1', '.V2', '.V3'), n(), replace=T)) %>% #create another identifier with periods
  mutate(ID_2 = paste(ID, ID_2, sep='')) -> df

# each person has 5 observations
df <- rbind(df, df, df, df, df)

#generate y values
df %>%
  mutate(y = rnorm(n(), person_mean, sd=.5)) -> test.dat

#two models fit on the same data. Only difference is the format of the grouping variable (ID vs ID_2)
m_test <- brm(y ~ group*condition + (1|gr(ID, by=group)), data=test.dat)
m_test2 <- brm(y ~ group*condition + (1|gr(ID_2, by=group)), data=test.dat)

truncated...

#m_test
Group-Level Effects:
~ID (Number of levels: 200)
                     Estimate Est.Error l-95% CI u-95% CI Eff.Sample Rhat
sd(Intercept:groupA)     0.50      0.04     0.43     0.60        835 1.01
sd(Intercept:groupB)     2.18      0.16     1.91     2.53        330 1.01

......

#m_test2
Group-Level Effects:
~ID_2 (Number of levels: 200)
                     Estimate Est.Error l-95% CI u-95% CI Eff.Sample Rhat
sd(Intercept:groupA)     1.68      0.13     1.46     1.96        219 1.02
sd(Intercept:groupB)     1.49      0.12     1.28     1.74        312 1.01

If I'm wrong, I would appreciate understanding the reason for this difference.

