Currently, vectorization is not implemented and is hard to do in general as user-defined Stan functions cannot be overloaded (that is differnet function signatures defined on the same function name). If I get a good idea how to allow vectorization, I will implement it.

2018-03-29 16:25 GMT+02:00 Lucas Deschamps <lucas.de...@gmail.com>:
> I'm actually trying your wonderful new function, and a question appears : did you implement a way to vectorize the sampling statement? The resulting code is now :
>
>   // likelihood including all constants
>   if (!prior_only) {
>     for (n in 1:N) {
>       target += gamma2_lpdf(Y[n] | mu[n], phi);
>     }
>   }
>
> Which is known to be slower. But I can guess that to ensure compatibility with all possible strange distributions, it is simpler to use a loop!
>
> Anyway, great job!

>
> Le jeudi 29 mars 2018 06:06:00 UTC-4, Paul Buerkner a écrit :

> > In the latest github version of brms, you can define new response distributions yourself using the custom_family() function.
> >
> > See https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/blob/master/vignettes/brms_customfamilies.Rmd for more details
> >
> > Am Dienstag, 6. März 2018 19:58:20 UTC+1 schrieb Paul Buerkner:

> > > No solution that I am aware of. In the parameterization I use in brms, mu is the mean, but as you say, the precision depends on the mean.
> > >
> > > The only solution would be to add a new family, gamma2() say, that uses your parameterization. You may comment on https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms/issues/110 so that I don't lose track of it.

> > > 2018-03-06 19:53 GMT+01:00 Lucas Deschamps <lucas.de...@gmail.com>:

> > > > Hello!
> > > >
> > > > I'm used to model jointly mean and dispersion for strictly positive variables. To do this, I describe response variable by a gamma distribution parametrized by the two orthogonal terms mu (mean) and phi (precision). That is, in stan code :
> > > >
> > > >
> > > >   shape = mu .* mu ./ phi;
> > > >   rate = mu ./ phi;
> > > >
> > > >      y ~ gamma(shape, rate);
> > > >
> > > > Because I have to use splines to produce a GAMM for a particular problem, I found far more convenient to use brms instead of rstan. Unfortunatly, the gamma disribution is only parametrized in term of mu and shape, with the following formulation I found in the code created by brms :
> > > >
> > > > mu[n] = shape[n] * exp(-(mu[n]));
> > > >
> > > > However, in that case, the precision is not independant of the mean.
> > > >
> > > > Is there any trick that would allow me to use the formulation with mu and phi, but also to keep the advantages provided by brmsfit objects??
> > > >
> > > > Thank you very much!
> > > > Lucas
> > > >

